# Case Study: Autonomous Patient Triage & Scheduling Agent Governance
**System Characterization:** Regional Hospital Network Patient Portal Deployment

### 1. Pre-Deployment: Alignment & Intake Architecture
Business Objectives: Optimization of patient routing, automated specialist scheduling, and proactive lab ordering pipelines.
Data Minimization & Notice: Implemented granular, just-in-time consent models displaying strict processing parameters with unambiguous opt-out alternatives.
Comprehensive Data Inventory: Mapping and cataloging of all unstructured inputs, electronic health record (EHR) integrations, and telemetry payloads.

### 2. Algorithmic Risk & Impact Assessments
Framework Alignment:Evaluated under the NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF) and OECD AI Principles.
Vulnerability Analysis:Active stress-testing against prompt injection vectors, unauthorized function calls, model drift, and disparate impact bias in triage urgency scores.

### 3. Technical Guardrails & Runtime Governance
Access Architecture: Strict enforcement of Least Privilege access controls and multi-tenant isolation boundaries for external insurance/pharmacy API endpoints.
Operational Guardrails: Human-in-the-loop validation checkpoints for high-risk autonomous outputs (e.g., routing critical emergency alerts).

### 4. Continuous Post-Deployment Monitoring
Performance Metrics: Real-time tracking of hallucination thresholds accounting for robustness, false-positive/negative triage scoring, bias audits and data distribution drift.
Security Baselines: Auditing data streams to maintain strict adherence to the CIA Triad (Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability).

### 5. Data Lifecycle, DSARs, & Incident Response
Data Retention & Hygiene: Automated purging protocols for interaction logs upon patient service termination to guarantee HIPAA alignment.
DSAR Execution Management: Human-audited workflow pipelines to retrieve explicit reasoning traces and system logs without degrading core healthcare system retention requirements.
Fail-Safe Protocol (The Kill Switch):Immediate automated revocation of agent autonomy upon crossing anomaly thresholds, forcing an instantaneous fallback to a deterministic, rule-based infrastructure.
